"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][6]={"school_physics_en:M6-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M6-L1 – Position, distance, time and reference frames<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Motion and speed<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A train enters a station. To people on the platform it moves; to a\npassenger the seat beside them stays at the same position. Both\nobservations can be correct because they use different reference\nframes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>To describe motion, first say <strong>relative to what<\/strong> it is\nmeasured. A <strong>reference frame<\/strong> includes a reference\nobject, a position axis and a clock. For now we use straight-line motion\nalong an oriented\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-axis.\n<strong>Position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nis the coordinate at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits sign depends on the chosen origin and positive direction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Displacement<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=x_2-x_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand can be positive or negative. <strong>Distance travelled<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadds the lengths of the path segments and is non-negative. If an object\ntravels along a straight line without turning back,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=|\\Delta x|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\notherwise distance is larger. Time instants\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nare different from elapsed time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=t_2-t_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSI units are metres\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nand seconds\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A position axis showing\nan outward and return journey, with separate markers for distance and\ndisplacement.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_031_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Position scale from 2 m to 9 m and back to 5 m: distance 11 m, displacement +3 m.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Position scale from 2 m to 9 m and back\nto 5 m: distance 11 m, displacement +3 m.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Distance adds both path segments; displacement compares only\nstart and finish.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A student starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=2\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwalks to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=9\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand returns to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=5-2=3\\,\\mathrm{m},\\qquad s=(9-2)+(9-5)=11\\,\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The movement lasts from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_1=4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_2=14\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=10\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not an error: they are different quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom activity:<\/strong> Mark a two-metre scale\nalong an unobstructed floor section. Mark start, turn and finish and\ntime the movement with a stopwatch. Compute distance from path segments\nand displacement from coordinates. Do not block exits or routes used by\nothers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How is position different from distance travelled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object moves from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-2\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=6\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout turning. Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A child walks\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nforward, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nback. Find distance and displacement.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What information must be defined to describe motion in a reference\nframe?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Position is a coordinate at one instant; distance sums the lengths\nof the path segments.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=6-(-2)=8\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=8\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=+1\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith forward taken as positive.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A reference object and oriented position axis, plus time measured\nwith a clock.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The model is restricted to straight-line motion. Curved paths require\nmore than one coordinate. Here different reference frames are treated as\nsimple observer viewpoints; relativistic effects are outside the\nscope.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Motion, speed,\nacceleration, motion laws and diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/lineare-bewegung-gleichungen/grundwissen/gleichfoermige-bewegungen\">LEIFIphysik\n– Uniform motions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Position–time law, constant velocity and unit\nconversion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Educational standards in Germany<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2024 lower-secondary\nschool-leaving standards – mechanics competencies · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M6-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M6-L2 – Average speed and unit conversion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Motion and speed<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two people cover the same 600-metre distance, but one takes two\nminutes and the other three. Average speed lets us compare their overall\npace.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Average speed<\/strong> is the total distance travelled\ndivided by the elapsed time:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar v_{\\mathrm{speed}}=\\frac{s}{\\Delta t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For directed straight-line motion, <strong>average velocity<\/strong>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar v_x=\\Delta x/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has a sign. The distinction matters when an object reverses\ndirection: an outward-and-return trip can have a large total distance\nbut zero average velocity. Everyday use of “speed” and “velocity” is\nsometimes loose, so we will state which quantity is meant.<\/p>\n<p>The SI unit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{km}=1000\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{h}=3600\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3.6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{m/s}=3.6\\,\\mathrm{km/h},\\quad 1\\,\\mathrm{km/h}=\\frac{1}{3.6}\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Two distance-and-time\ncomparisons and a unit conversion for the same scenario.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_032_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two 600-m trips take 120 s and 180 s; average speeds are 5.0 and 3.33 m/s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two 600-m trips take 120 s and 180 s;\naverage speeds are 5.0 and 3.33 m/s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The same distance completed in less time implies higher average\nspeed.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A cyclist covers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar v=\\frac{600\\,\\mathrm{m}}{120\\,\\mathrm{s}}=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}=18\\,\\mathrm{km/h}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the same distance takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>180<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe average speed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.33\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe calculation uses the full distance and total time, not the highest\ninstantaneous speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom activity:<\/strong> Measure a straight\nten-metre distance in a gym. One learner walks at a steady pace while\nanother measures time. Repeat a few times and compare the readings;\nstart timing and human reaction introduce uncertainty. Do not run or\nmeasure traffic speeds on roads.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A bicycle covers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind average speed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">54\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the average velocity for a complete trip that returns to the\nstarting point?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar v=300/60=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>28.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\times3.6=28.8\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>54<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">54/3.6=15\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Zero because the total displacement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neven though average speed can be positive.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>An average does not tell us the highest speed, short stops or changes\nalong the route. A simple arithmetic mean of segment speeds is not\ngenerally the overall average. Measurements have uncertainties.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Motion, speed,\nacceleration, motion laws and diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/lineare-bewegung-gleichungen/grundwissen/gleichfoermige-bewegungen\">LEIFIphysik\n– Uniform motions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Position–time law, constant velocity and unit\nconversion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Educational standards in Germany<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2024 lower-secondary\nschool-leaving standards – mechanics competencies · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M6-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M6-L3 – Uniform straight-line motion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Motion and speed<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A person moves with constant velocity along a straight path. In each\nadditional second they cover the same displacement. A straight line\nappears on the position–time graph.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>uniform straight-line motion<\/strong> the directed\nvelocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. For starting position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x_0+v_x t.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>slope<\/strong> of the line on a <strong>position–time\ngraph<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x/\\Delta t=v_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA horizontal line represents rest, while a line with negative slope\nrepresents motion in the negative direction. Only when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the object travels in the positive direction can we simply write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=vt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor distance. A <strong>distance–time graph<\/strong> instead shows total\ndistance, which cannot decrease. Keeping the two graphs distinct avoids\nmistakes when direction changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Two lines on an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph with a marked initial position and slope triangle.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_033_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Straight line from x = 2 m at t = 0 s to x = 8 m at t = 4 s, slope 1.5 m/s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Straight line from x = 2 m at t = 0 s to\nx = 8 m at t = 4 s, slope 1.5 m/s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The slope of a straight position–time line gives constant\ndirected velocity.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A cart is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand moves steadily at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter four seconds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(4\\,\\mathrm{s})=2\\,\\mathrm{m}+1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\cdot4\\,\\mathrm{s}=8\\,\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The graph points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0\\,\\mathrm{s},2\\,\\mathrm{m})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4\\,\\mathrm{s},8\\,\\mathrm{m})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefine the straight line; its slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8-2)/4=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Roll a toy cart across a smooth table\nor low ramp and mark positions at equal time intervals using a short\nvideo. Check whether the points lie approximately on a straight line.\nFriction may slow the cart, so uniform motion is only an\napproximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the slope of a position–time line mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object moves at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=3\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a horizontal position–time line show?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a negative slope mean for an oriented\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-axis?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The constant directed velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3+2\\cdot5=13\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Position stays constant: rest in this reference frame.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Motion in the negative axis direction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>An approximately straight measured line does not prove exactly\nconstant velocity; uncertainty and sampling may hide small changes. When\nacceleration occurs, the graph is no longer straight.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Motion, speed,\nacceleration, motion laws and diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/lineare-bewegung-gleichungen/grundwissen/gleichfoermige-bewegungen\">LEIFIphysik\n– Uniform motions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Position–time law, constant velocity and unit\nconversion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/lineare-bewegung-gleichungen/grundwissen/zusammenhang-der-diagramme\">LEIFIphysik\n– Relations between motion graphs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Position–time slopes and\nvelocity–time areas · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Educational standards in Germany<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2024 lower-secondary\nschool-leaving standards – mechanics competencies · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M6-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M6-L4 – Acceleration and deceleration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Motion and speed<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A scooter starts at a light and gains speed. Later the rider brakes.\nBoth actions change the velocity and can be described using\nacceleration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Average acceleration<\/strong> in straight-line motion is the\nchange in directed velocity divided by elapsed time:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a_x=\\frac{v_{x,2}-v_{x,1}}{t_2-t_1},\\qquad [a]=\\mathrm{m/s^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the direction of travel is positive, positive acceleration\ninitially increases velocity in that direction. A negative acceleration\nwhen velocity is positive represents <strong>deceleration<\/strong>.\nNegative acceleration does not always mean slowing down: an object\ntravelling left with negative velocity can speed up if its acceleration\nis also negative.<\/p>\n<p>For <strong>constant<\/strong> acceleration,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x(t)=v_{x,0}+a_x t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe must state that assumption whenever we use the relation.\n<strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Velocity–time lines for\nspeeding up, cruising and braking.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_034_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Velocity rises from 2 to 8 m/s over 3 s, then falls to zero over 4 s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Velocity rises from 2 to 8 m/s over 3 s,\nthen falls to zero over 4 s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Slope on a velocity–time graph represents acceleration.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A cart accelerates uniformly in the positive direction from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a=\\frac{8-2}{3}=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It then brakes from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto rest in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a=(0-8)/4=-2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two accelerations have equal magnitude but opposite directions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Examine a short video of a slowly\nmoving toy cart. Estimate velocities for successive intervals, then\ncalculate average acceleration from their differences. Use low speeds on\nan unobstructed tabletop; do not carry out risky vehicle\nexperiments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI unit of acceleration?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object goes from rest to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object moving in the positive direction slows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can an object speed up with negative acceleration?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Metres per second squared:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a=(6-0)/3=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar a=(4-10)/2=-3\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes, if velocity and acceleration both point in the negative\ndirection.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Real-world acceleration often varies with time. Average acceleration\ndoes not specify its instantaneous value. Motion along a curve can\ninvolve acceleration even at constant speed, but that is beyond this\nlesson.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Motion, speed,\nacceleration, motion laws and diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/beschleunigte-bewegung/grundwissen/beschleunigung-bei-gleichmaessig-beschleunigter-bewegung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Constant acceleration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Acceleration as the change in velocity per\nunit time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Educational standards in Germany<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2024 lower-secondary\nschool-leaving standards – mechanics competencies · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M6-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M6-L5 – Reading and drawing motion graphs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Motion and speed<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two graphs can look similar while describing different physics. The\nvertical-axis quantity is what determines their meaning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>position–time\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ngraph<\/strong> plots position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives average velocity. A steep rising line means a large positive\nvelocity; a falling line means negative velocity. For a curved graph, a\nlocal tangent slope gives instantaneous velocity.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>velocity–time\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ngraph<\/strong> plots directed velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta v_x/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives average acceleration. The <strong>signed area between the graph\nand the time axis<\/strong> gives displacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAreas below the axis count negatively. Total distance travelled is the\nsum of the magnitudes of the separate areas.<\/p>\n<p>Graphs need labelled axes, units and appropriate scales.\n<strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A linear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph beside a constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph, with a slope triangle and a shaded area.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_035_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Position–time line from 0 to 12 m over 4 s and velocity–time rectangle at 3 m/s for 4 s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Position–time line from 0 to 12 m over 4\ns and velocity–time rectangle at 3 m/s for 4 s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Slope on a position–time graph gives velocity; area under\nvelocity–time gives displacement.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na velocity–time graph shows constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe area is a rectangle:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\cdot4\\,\\mathrm{s}=12\\,\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>On the matching position–time graph with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nposition rises linearly from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{m}/4\\,\\mathrm{s}=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf a velocity–time graph has an upward-sloping line, its slope instead\nrepresents acceleration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Make a table of four position\nmeasurements at one-second intervals and plot the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph on squared paper. Calculate successive slopes and make a simple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph. Do not conceal measurement uncertainty with overly precise drawn\nlines.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the slope of an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph show?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the slope of a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph show?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind displacement from the area.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object moves for 2 s at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 2 s at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind displacement and distance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Directed velocity: average from a secant slope or instantaneous from\na tangent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Acceleration: average from a secant slope or instantaneous from a\ntangent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=4\\times5=20\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Displacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+6-6=0\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6+6=12\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Areas on velocity–time graphs only have physical meaning if axis\nunits are correct. A curve fitted to uncertain measurements is a model,\nnot exact ground truth. The diagram uses simplified noise-free\nmotion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Motion, speed,\nacceleration, motion laws and diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/lineare-bewegung-gleichungen/grundwissen/zusammenhang-der-diagramme\">LEIFIphysik\n– Relations between motion graphs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Position–time slopes and\nvelocity–time areas · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/lineare-bewegung-gleichungen/grundwissen/zeit-geschwindigkeit-diagramm\">LEIFIphysik\n– Velocity–time diagram<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Constant and changing velocity plotted\nagainst time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Educational standards in Germany<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2024 lower-secondary\nschool-leaving standards – mechanics competencies · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M6-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M6-L6 – Motion and road safety<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Motion and speed<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A vehicle travels over the same surface first at a low speed, then at\ntwice that speed. Why might stopping require far more than twice the\ndistance?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>In a simplified model <strong>stopping distance<\/strong> is the sum\nof <strong>reaction distance<\/strong> and <strong>braking\ndistance<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{\\mathrm{stop}}=s_{\\mathrm{reaction}}+s_{\\mathrm{brake}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At constant speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring an assumed reaction time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{\\mathrm{reaction}}=v_0t_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a constant braking deceleration magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{\\mathrm{brake}}=\\frac{v_0^2}{2b}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under the same conditions reaction distance grows directly with\nspeed, but braking distance grows with its <strong>square<\/strong>.\nDoubling speed therefore doubles reaction distance and quadruples\nbraking distance in the model. These are model relationships, not\nguaranteed stopping distances on a real road. <strong>Original teaching\ndiagram:<\/strong> Reaction and braking segments compared at two initial\nspeeds.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_036_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"At 10 m/s, reaction and braking distances are 10 m each; at 20 m/s they are 20 m and 40 m.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">At 10 m/s, reaction and braking distances\nare 10 m each; at 20 m/s they are 20 m and 40 m.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The model shows linear growth of reaction distance and quadratic\ngrowth of braking distance under fixed assumptions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Illustrative model, not driving advice:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r=1.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconstant deceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a flat dry surface. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_r=10\\,\\mathrm{m},\\qquad s_b=\\frac{10^2}{2\\cdot5}=10\\,\\mathrm{m},\\qquad s_a=20\\,\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same assumptions:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_r=20\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_b=40\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_a=60\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese speeds correspond to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>36<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>72<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">72\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper or computer model only:<\/strong> Vary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a table and plot stopping distance. Never test braking performance\nwith vehicles, bicycles or people for this activity. Discuss how wet\nsurfaces, distraction, gradients, tyres and load can change real\noutcomes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the two parts of stopping distance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find reaction distance for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=12\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r=1.5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does model braking distance change when speed doubles, with\nother conditions unchanged?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=15\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=7.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reaction distance and braking distance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_r=12\\times1.5=18\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It becomes four times as large, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_b\\propto v_0^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>225<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_b=225/15=15\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Constant braking deceleration and fixed reaction time are strong\nassumptions. The model ignores vehicle-system delays and varying\nfriction. Numbers are illustrative, not assured braking or safe\nfollowing distances. Actual road safety rules and conservative margins\ntake precedence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1 – Forces as interactions and\nhow to represent them<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Motion, speed,\nacceleration, motion laws and diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/beschleunigte-bewegung/ausblick/anhalteweg\">LEIFIphysik\n– Stopping distance<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Reaction, braking and stopping distance within\na physical model · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/beschleunigte-bewegung/grundwissen/beschleunigung-bei-gleichmaessig-beschleunigter-bewegung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Constant acceleration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Acceleration as the change in velocity per\nunit time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Educational standards in Germany<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 2024 lower-secondary\nschool-leaving standards – mechanics competencies · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
